Formalizing "any minimizer equals the ray": If P is an LPath from 0 to x with P.length = |x|²/(2√τ) (i.e., a minimizer), then... the completing-square inequality shows ∫ √σ|P.velocity - (1/√σ)c|² dσ = 0 where c = (1/(2√τ))x. Since the integrand is ≥ 0 and continuous-ish... Actually the integrand √σ |v(σ) - (1/√σ)c|² is nonneg and integrable (by P.integrable_energy and integrable_cross), and its integral is 0, hence it is 0 a.e. — via `MeasureTheory.integral_eq_zero_iff_of_nonneg` or rather we have ∫ f = 0 and f ≥ 0 a.e. and f integrable → f = 0 a.e. Mathlib has `integral_eq_zero_iff_of_nonneg_ae`. Then for a.e. σ, P.velocity σ = (1/√σ)c. Then by FTC on the interval: P.curve σ = ∫... hmm, but "a.e. equality" of the derivative plus continuity gives pointwise equality of the curve with the ray: curve(σ) = 2√σ c = (√σ/√τ)x for all σ ∈ [0,τ]. That's a nice unconditional theorem: "every L-minimizer from the origin is the straight ray γ(σ) = (√σ/√τ)x".

Wait — actually is that right? In flat space the minimizer is unique: yes, L-length is strictly convex; the only minimizer from 0 to x is the straight line. And indeed the equality case of the completing-the-square forces γ' ≡ (1/√σ)c a.e.

Hmm, but careful: for the equality case we need P.length = |x|²/(2√τ). The nonneg integral argument: from gaussian_length_le we have ∫ f dσ ≥ 0 where f = the completing-square integrand = √σ|v - c/√σ|² (shown equal in hnonneg's hid). In the equality case, ∫ f = 0. Then f = 0 a.e. Then √σ > 0 on (0,τ] gives v = c/√σ a.e. Then integrating: curve(σ) = ∫_0^σ v ds = 2√σ c. 

