Hmm. But we can derive it: |v|² = ⟪v,v⟫ = F.metric σ (v) (v) — integrable_energy gives √σ·⟪v,v⟫ integrable on [0,τ]. On [σ₀, τ] for any σ₀ > 0, we have √σ ≥ √σ₀ > 0, so ⟪v,v⟫ ≤ (1/√σ₀)·(√σ ⟪v,v⟫)… that gives integrability of |v|² on [σ₀,τ], hence of v on [σ₀,τ]. But near 0 the velocity blows up like 1/√σ; we don't have integrability of v near 0 as a field... but the equality v = c/√σ a.e. gives it a posteriori: after we derive v = c/√σ a.e. on [0,τ], we can prove v is integrable on [0,σ] by comparing with 1/√σ which is integrable (intervalIntegrable_one_div_sqrt). 

