- So a.e. σ: √σ ⟪v - (1/√σ)c, v - (1/√σ)c⟫ = 0 → ‖v - (1/√σ)c‖² = 0 → v = (1/√σ)c.
- Then show v is IntervalIntegrable on 0 σ: v =ᵐ[volume on Icc 0 σ] (1/√σ)c... actually the a.e. equality is on the whole [0,τ] (with the caveat: the equality v = c/√σ holds for a.e. σ in the interval measure restricted to Icc 0 τ; but at σ=0 the point has measure zero anyway; c/√σ is integrable on [0,σ] by intervalIntegrable_one_div_sqrt). Using `IntervalIntegrable.congr_ae`... need the a.e. equality with respect to volume restricted to the interval — that's exactly what we have.
