Then conclude: for all σ ∈ Icc 0 τ, P.curve σ = (√σ/√τ)•x. That's "every L-minimizer from the origin is the straight ray". The theorem statement: `IsLMinimizer P → ∀ σ ∈ Set.Icc 0 τ, P.curve σ = gaussianPath x τ σ`. Where gaussianPath is the D7 ray (√σ/√τ)•x. To be self-contained in D11, I can define my own `euclideanRay`. Actually reusing D7's `gaussianPath` is fine since D11 imports D7 (item 3 requires referencing the D7 interface anyway).

Hmm, but actually should D11 re-derive the L-length from scratch using the D10 heat kernel? Item 1: "Using the D10 heat kernel on ℝⁿ, define the L-length of a curve γ(τ) as ∫ √τ (|γ'|² + R) dτ (R=0 in flat case) and the reduced distance from the heat-kernel asymptotics."

So item 1 wants: in D11, define the L-length using the D7 interface (or a D11-local definition?) and define the reduced distance **from the heat-kernel asymptotics**. The "reduced distance from the heat-kernel asymptotics" means: the heat kernel K(t, x) = (4πt)^{-n/2} exp(-|x|²/(4t)) can be written as (4πt)^{-n/2} exp(-ℓ) where ℓ = |x|²/(4t); i.e., define the reduced distance ℓ from the heat kernel: ℓ(x,τ) = -log((4πτ)^{n/2} K(τ,x)). In flat space this is the standard way Perelman's ℓ appears from the heat-kernel asymptotic: K ~ (4πτ)^{-n/2} exp(-ℓ). So define `reducedDistanceFromHeatKernel n τ x = -Real.log ((4 * π * τ) ^ ((n:ℝ)/2) * gaussianKernel n τ x)` and prove it equals |x|²/(4τ). That's a clean heat-kernel-asymptotics definition of the reduced distance, and then prove it agrees with the L-length minimizer reduced distance ℓ = |x|²/(4τ) computed in D7.

